198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFor positive integer $n$ such that $n < 10{,}000$, the number $n+2005$ has exactly 21 positive factors. What is the sum of all the possible values of $n$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"414818625158cf3790c09f3fe461ac69eedd962542ebfd1ac29f783686e0b0b0"},"seconds":36.38997006416321,"seed":9439090786621895,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to determine the possible values of \\( n \\) such that \\( n + 2005 \\) has exactly 21 positive factors. We start by analyzing the factorization of 21. The number 21 can be factored into positive integers in the following ways:\n\\[ 21 = 21 \\times 1 \\]\n\\[ 21 = 7 \\times 3 \\]\n\nThis means the number \\( n + 2005 \\) must be of the form \\( p^{20} \\) or \\( p_1^2 p_2^2 \\), where \\( p \\) and \\( p_1, p_2 \\) are prime numbers.\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 2005 = p^{20} \\)\nWe need to find a prime number \\( p \\) such that \\( p^{20} - 2005 \\) is a positive integer less than 10,000. We check the smallest primes:\n- For \\( p = 2 \\):\n  \\[ 2^{20} = 1048576 \\]\n  \\[ 2^{20} - 2005 = 1048576 - 2005 = 1046571 \\]\n  This is greater than 10,000, so \\( p = 2 \\) is not a solution.\n- For \\( p = 3 \\):\n  \\[ 3^{20} = 3486784401 \\]\n  This is much greater than 10,000, so \\( p = 3 \\) is not a solution.\n- For \\( p = 5 \\):\n  \\[ 5^{20} = 95367431640625 \\]\n  This is much greater than 10,000, so \\( p = 5 \\) is not a solution.\n\nSince \\( p^{20} \\) grows very quickly, there are no solutions for \\( p \\geq 2 \\). Therefore, there are no solutions in this case.\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 2005 = p_1^2 p_2^2 \\)\nWe need to find pairs of prime numbers \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) such that \\( p_1^2 p_2^2 - 2005 \\) is a positive integer less than 10,000. We check the smallest prime pairs:\n- For \\( p_1 = 2 \\) and \\( p_2 = 3 \\):\n  \\[ 2^2 \\times 3^2 = 4 \\times 9 = 36 \\]\n  \\[ 36 - 2005 = -1969 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 2 \\) and \\( p_2 = 5 \\):\n  \\[ 2^2 \\times 5^2 = 4 \\times 25 = 100 \\]\n  \\[ 100 - 2005 = -1905 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 2 \\) and \\( p_2 = 7 \\):\n  \\[ 2^2 \\times 7^2 = 4 \\times 49 = 196 \\]\n  \\[ 196 - 2005 = -1809 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 2 \\) and \\( p_2 = 11 \\):\n  \\[ 2^2 \\times 11^2 = 4 \\times 121 = 484 \\]\n  \\[ 484 - 2005 = -1521 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 3 \\) and \\( p_2 = 5 \\):\n  \\[ 3^2 \\times 5^2 = 9 \\times 25 = 225 \\]\n  \\[ 225 - 2005 = -1780 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 3 \\) and \\( p_2 = 7 \\):\n  \\[ 3^2 \\times 7^2 = 9 \\times 49 = 441 \\]\n  \\[ 441 - 2005 = -1564 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 5 \\) and \\( p_2 = 7 \\):\n  \\[ 5^2 \\times 7^2 = 25 \\times 49 = 1225 \\]\n  \\[ 1225 - 2005 = -780 \\]\n  This is not a positive integer.\n- For \\( p_1 = 7 \\) and \\( p_2 = 11 \\):\n  \\[ 7^2 \\times 11^2 = 49 \\times 121 = 5929 \\]\n  \\[ 5929 - 2005 = 3924 \\]\n  This is a positive integer and less than 10,000.\n\nThus, \\( n = 5929 - 2005 = 3924 \\).\n\nWe need to check if there are any other pairs of primes that satisfy the condition. We continue to check:\n- For \\( p_1 = 11 \\) and \\( p_2 = 13 \\):\n  \\[ 11^2 \\times 13^2 = 121 \\times 169 = 20449 \\]\n  \\[ 20449 - 2005 = 18444 \\]\n  This is greater than 10,000.\n\nNo other pairs of primes will satisfy the condition since \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) must be small enough for \\( p_1^2 p_2^2 < 10005 \\).\n\nTherefore, the only possible value of \\( n \\) is 3924. The sum of all possible values of \\( n \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3924} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{3924}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:79"}
